ry loader that asserts len==8 or similar).\n\n2. **Authority separation**: routing/join/acceptance/terminal decisions in the graph are made ONLY from a code-computed/deterministic value (not from anything a model response could set). Look for how \"pending_guard\" or similar control state is computed, and whether any model output schema is rejected if it tries to set control/routing fields (e.g. a FORBIDDEN_FIELDS check on model schemas).\n\n3. **Denominators recomputed fresh**: any \"how many members should this join wait for\" (unit denominator) computation is recomputed from ground truth on each call, not cached/trusted from a prior claim. And any join-completion check rejects extra, stale, or cross-unit members (fails closed rather than open).\n\n4. **Repair invariant**: the repair-admission code path recomputes its own diff (doesn't trust a model-supplied diff), rejects a stale parent hash, and hard-fails (does not silently ignore) any out-of-boundary file/pointer reference.\n\n5. **Persistence/resume**: uses a real LangGraph checkpointer (e.g. SqliteSaver, not an in-memory/fake one) for persistence. A resume-validation function refuses resume on drift across several classes (e.g. identity, frozen digest, executable/code version, evidence, already-accepted bytes) — find the function and enumerate what drift classes it actually checks. Also confirm resume is refused outside an allow-listed set of \"resumable terminal\" states.\n\n6. **Terminals**: enumerate exactly how many distinct terminal/outcome kinds exist. Find where a terminal is written — does it independently re-derive/validate the claimed terminal type rather than trust it blindly, and downgrade to a failure terminal if the claim doesn't hold up? Is there a \"paused\" terminal that's only allowed when the pending failure is specifically classified as a \"pause\" class (not any arbitrary failure)?\n\n7. **CLI single path**: in run_curriculum.py, is there exactly one function that actually executes a live run (not a dry-run/simulation), and do both the \"fresh episode\" and \"resume episode\" code branches call the SAME graph-building function (one production graph builder), each exactly once? Are there any reachable references to a simulation mode, a \"session bridge\", or a Plan-25/legacy controller from this file or from langgraph_factory/graph.py?\n\nFor each of the 7 items, give: PASS/FAIL, the file:line evidence, and 1-2 sentences of reasoning. Keep total response under 900 words. This is the whole task — do not attempt to fix anything, this is audit-only.","agentId":"af9c69384a7dde58b","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Audit Results\n\n**1. Eight model jobs — PASS**\n`runtime/langgraph_factory/transport.py:159` — `load_job_registry()`: `if declared != 8 or len(entries) != 8: raise RouteRejected(...)`. `MODEL_NODE_IDS` (`model_nodes.py:101-110`) lists exactly 8 job IDs. `resolve_prompt_path`/`resolve_schema_path` (`transport.py:200-216`) resolve prompt/schema files relative to package-owned `PROMPT_DIR`/`SCHEMA_DIR` and reject any path that escapes that directory. The registry loader enforces the exact count on every load, not just at authoring time.\n\n**2. Authority separation — PASS**\nRouting (`routing.py:304-338`) reads only a code-produced `pending_guard`/`pending_failure` record via `_guard_value`, never a model payload; `DYNAMIC_GUARDS` (`routing.py:263-271`) even rejects a stored destination that names a model node (`node_id.startswith(_MODEL_ID_PREFIXES)` → `RoutingViolation`). Model-node writes are allow-listed and enforced at `model_nodes.py:1260-1265` (`_assert_model_node_update`, checked against `MODEL_NODE_WRITABLE_FIELDS`, which excludes `pending_guard`, `terminal`, `artifact_heads`, `accepted_unit_receipts`, etc.). Note: `FORBIDDEN_MODEL_NODE_FIELDS` (`model_nodes.py:142`) is defined but never referenced anywhere in the codebase — dead code — but the actual enforcement mechanism is the allow-list, which is at least as strong.\n\n**3. Denominators recomputed fresh — PASS**\n`compute_unit_denominator()` (`acceptance.py:108-268`) is called independently by both `D16_REDUCE_UNIT_EVIDENCE` and `D22_ACCEPT_UNIT` (`acceptance.py:315-382`), each time rebuilt from current `artifact_heads`/`deterministic_checks`/`unit_reviews` state — no cached denominator is trusted. Join rejection fails closed: `D07_CORRELATE_AND_ADMIT_SOURCES` rejects cross-unit contamination (`sources.py:505-518`), extra members (`sources.py:522-525`), and stale interpretations whose parent hash no longer matches the current retrieval (`sources.py:527-540`). `D12_VISUAL_BARRIER_AND_JOIN` similarly rejects missing/extra members (`visuals.py:346-354`) and stale content-hash results (`visuals.py:356-366`).\n\n**4. Repair invariant — PASS**\n`D20_ADMIT_UNIT_REPAIR` (`repair.py:882-989`) recomputes its own diff via `json_pointer_diff(parent_body, new_body)` (line 944), never trusting a model-supplied manifest; rejects a stale parent hash at line 937-942 (`declared_parent_sha == current_hash`, else `SystemFailure`); and hard-fails any out-of-boundary pointer at line 946-952 (`require(not out_of_bound, \"integrity\", ...)`, which raises via `require()` → `SystemFailure`, not a silently-dropped finding).\n\n**5. Persistence/resume — PASS**\nReal `SqliteSaver` used at `persistence.py:540-545` (`from langgraph.checkpoint.sqlite import SqliteSaver`). `validate_resume_inputs()` (`persistence.py:1011-1031`) enforces 5 classified drift categories across 10 fields: `identity` (contract_version, run_id, created_at, engine_root, curriculum_root, active_manifest_path, output_root, mode, requested_unit_id), `frozen_digest`, `executable`, `evidence`, `accepted_bytes` — and an unclassified field itself refuses resume. `_validate_terminal_legality()` (`persistence.py:1034-1044`) refuses resume unless the prior terminal is in `RESUMABLE_TERMINALS = {\"INTERRUPTED\", \"PAUSED_PREREQUISITE\"}` (`persistence.py:196`).\n\n**6. Terminals — PASS**\nExactly 6 terminal kinds (`reducers.py:433-439`: UNIT_ACCEPTED, COMPLETE, INTERRUPTED, PAUSED_PREREQUISITE, CONVERGENCE_EXHAUSTED, SYSTEM_FAILURE). `write_terminal()` (`nodes/terminal.py:472-500`) is the sole terminal-writing implementation; it calls `validate_terminal_candidate()` (`nodes/terminal.py:396-415`), which independently re-derives each kind's precondition from live state (`_validate_unit_accepted`, `_validate_complete`, etc.) without consulting the proposing guard, and downgrades to `SYSTEM_FAILURE` on any rejection (`_rejection_terminal`, line 446-469). `_validate_paused` (`nodes/terminal.py:307-327`) specifically rejects a PAUSED_PREREQUISITE candidate unless `pending_failure[\"class\"] == \"pause\"` (lines 321-326).\n\n**7. CLI single path — PASS**\n`run_curriculum.py` has exactly one live-execution function, `_run_live` (line 527); `_run_preflight` (line 318) is explicitly read-only and creates no run. Both the resume branch (line 554) and fresh branch (line 570) call `build_curriculum_factory_graph` exactly once each, the same function imported from `langgraph_factory.graph`. No reachable reference to simulation mode or `session_bridge` exists in `run_curriculum.py` or `graph.py` (grep confirms zero matches). A legacy `runtime/curriculum_factory_graph.py` module exists (`GRAPH_PACKAGE = \"plans/25_curriculum_factory_graph\"`) but is not imported by either file — it is dead/unreachable from this entry point, not a second live path."}],"resolvedModel":"claude-sonnet-5","totalDurationMs":111539,"totalTokens":128331,"totalToolUseCount":35,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":410,"cache_read_input_tokens":125823,"output_tokens":2096,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":410},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2096,